  <data key="d37">Higher crude oil prices raise transportation and production costs and retail fuel prices, which feed quickly into headline consumer price indices through energy and upstream cost passthrough.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">middle_east_ir_escalatio</data>
  <data key="d40">global_inflation_bond_st</data>
</edge>
<edge source="middle_east_ir_escalatio::oil_price_level" target="global_inflation_bond_st::ten_year_bond_yield" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">Sustained increases in oil push up inflation expectations and term premia, prompting investors to demand higher nominal yields on ten‑year bonds to compensate for expected tighter monetary policy and higher real/inflation risk.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">middle_east_ir_escalatio</data>
  <data key="d40">global_inflation_bond_st</data>
</edge>
<edge source="middle_east_ir_escalatio::oil_price_level" target="russia_ukraine_war::military_coercion_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">A sustained rise in global oil prices driven by Middle East escalation increases Russia's export revenues and fiscal space, directly enabling larger, longer or more intensive military operations in Ukraine through greater procurement, logistics and personnel sustainment.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">middle_east_ir_escalatio</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
