<edge source="transatlantic_security_s::sanction_pressure_level" target="transatlantic_security_s::energy_vuln" id="0">
  <data key="d28">intra_causal</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
</edge>
<edge source="transatlantic_security_s::sanction_pressure_level" target="transatlantic_security_s::info_polarization" id="0">
  <data key="d28">intra_causal</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
</edge>
<edge source="transatlantic_security_s::sanction_pressure_level" target="global_inflation_bond_st::broad_currency_index" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Escalating transatlantic financial sanctions divert trade and capital flows away from sanctioned currencies and toward safe‑haven currencies, causing a measurable appreciation of the major safe‑haven currency index.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">transatlantic_security_s</data>
  <data key="d40">global_inflation_bond_st</data>
</edge>
<edge source="transatlantic_security_s::sanction_pressure_level" target="middle_east_ir_escalatio::infra_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Sharper transatlantic sanctions on regime-linked actors increase incentives for those actors or their proxies to conduct asymmetric reprisals targeting energy infrastructure in the region as a means of imposing costs and signalling deterrence.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">transatlantic_security_s</data>
  <data key="d40">middle_east_ir_escalatio</data>
</edge>
<edge source="transatlantic_security_s::sanction_pressure_level" target="russia_ukraine_war::military_coercion_level" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Immediate escalation of broad, severe sanctions on Russia increases economic and regime pressure that can prompt retaliatory kinetic escalation or attempts to change facts on the ground to break sanctions momentum.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">transatlantic_security_s</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="transatlantic_security_s::sanction_pressure_level" target="russia_ukraine_war::energy_supply_disruption" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Targeted sanctions on Russian energy sectors or financial links incentivize Moscow to restrict exports or undertake countermeasures (e.g., pipeline throttling or delivery cutoffs), causing acute energy supply interruptions in the conflict theater and adjacent markets.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">transatlantic_security_s</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="transatlantic_security_s::sanction_pressure_level" target="us_china_tech_rivalry::reg_scrutiny_intensity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">An uptick in sanctions and related financial measures increases regulators' workload and incentives to open compliance audits and enforcement actions focused on AI‑chip suppliers and intermediaries to prevent sanctions evasion.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">transatlantic_security_s</data>
  <data key="d40">us_china_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
